# gitleaks findings ACCEPTED by exact fingerprint
# (<commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>). One line per finding; nothing else is
# suppressed.
#
# THIS FILE IS MEANT TO BECOME EMPTY. It exists only to carry one immutable
# historical commit, and there is a specific point at which it can be deleted.
# That point is described below, so nobody has to re-derive it.
#
# ROOT CAUSE — a skill NAME, not a digest format.
# gitleaks' generic-api-key rule needs a KEYWORD beside a high-entropy value.
# Exactly one line in `skills.lock` supplied one, and it was the skill's own
# name: `"cms-platform/cms-platform-secrets": "<64-hex>"`. The word "secrets"
# was the whole trigger. cms-platform itself never saw this — its lock lists
# only `adam/*` skills — so the repo that chose the name is not the repo that
# broke.
#
# TWO FIXES LANDED UPSTREAM, AND NEITHER ONE CAN CLEAN HISTORY.
#   1. agentskills#87 labels lock digests `sha256:<hex>`; `:` falls outside
#      the rule's capture class, truncating the capture below its 10-char floor.
#   2. The skill is renamed to `consumer-repo-provisioning`, removing the
#      keyword outright — the durable fix, since it needs no allowlist anywhere
#      and immunises every consumer at once rather than one repo at a time.
# Both change the WORKING TREE. The commit that introduced the old line is
# immutable, and `secrets-scan.yml` scans `base..head` on a pull request but
# THE FULL REACHABLE HISTORY on push, schedule and dispatch. That asymmetry is
# why adamdaniel.ai#3215's own green `scan` job proved nothing about the lane that
# was actually red.
#
# WHEN TO DELETE THIS FILE — not before, or main goes red again.
#   1. The rename has landed and this repo's `skills.lock` no longer contains
#      any gitleaks keyword. Check: the lock key must not match
#      access|auth|api|credential|creds|key|passwd|password|secret|token.
#   2. This repo's history has been rewritten to remove the offending line, all
#      refs pointing at the old commits are gone (branches, tags, AND pull
#      requests), and every existing clone has been RE-CLONED rather than
#      pulled — a stale clone that pulls will merge the old commits straight
#      back in.
#   3. Only then: empty this file and verify with a `workflow_dispatch` run of
#      `secrets-scan.yml` on the branch. That is the only pre-merge lane that
#      reads full history; a pull request's own scan cannot test it.
#
# A fingerprint rather than a blanket allowlist, deliberately: it pins one
# finding in one commit on one line under one rule, so a FUTURE bare-hex or
# keyword-bearing lock line — which would mean a real regression — still fails.
